-- The prototype-1 receive contract on the STM32H753 (D648): USART3 on PD8
-- and PD9 received into a ring by DMA1 stream 0, which DMAMUX1's channel 0
-- paces with usart3_rx_dma, request 45 (Table 124, p. 697).  Its public
-- interface and its atoms are the synthetic driver's and the RP2040
-- board's, with the same order of checks; its device protocol is this
-- chip's, from RM0433 Rev 9.
--
-- The stream counts down and, in normal mode, stops at zero with EN clear
-- (15.3.15, p. 654); it has no ring address, and a circular stream
-- reloads its count where no snapshot can see how often.  So an epoch's
-- budget is taken in laps of the ring's size, the last one shorter when
-- the budget is not a multiple of it, and the stream's transfer-complete
-- interrupt arms each next lap from the ring's start until the budget is
-- reached.  A snapshot reads the stream with interrupts masked: the bytes
-- of the laps completed, and of the lap armed what its count says, or all
-- of it when its completion is still pending.  Stop clears EN with the
-- completion interrupt, so that no lap is armed after it, and waits at
-- most 1000 polls for EN to read 0, which 15.3.16 (p. 654) says it does
-- once the transfer in progress completes; the count then holds what was
-- not transferred.  Bytes received after the last snapshot before a stop
-- are discarded with the epoch.
--
-- USART3 runs in FIFO mode (48.5.4, p. 2023), so its transmitter holds
-- 16 bytes and the echo keeps the line busy across the work between two
-- reads (D648).  While an epoch is exhausted or stopped, or between a lap
-- and the next, the receiver holds 17 bytes, 16 in its RXFIFO and one in
-- the receive data register (48.5.4, p. 2023; Table 402, p. 2018; note 2,
-- p. 2067), and the next is lost: the USART sets ORE (48.5.6, p. 2030),
-- and a snapshot reports that as an overrun, so no byte is lost
-- unreported.  The program wakes for a lap still in progress on SysTick's
-- millisecond and on USART3's IDLE interrupt (D649).
--
-- TRBUFF, DMA_SxCR's bit 20, is set, as RM0433 says it must be for a
-- stream that serves a USART (15.5.5, p. 662); the SVD omits the field and
-- the policy adds it (D652).
--
-- The ring is memory the stream writes behind the CPU's caches, so every
-- routine that hands it to the stream or reads it takes `core/cache`
-- maintenance evidence and does D227's handoff itself (D978): `open`,
-- `restart` and `configure` clean and invalidate the whole ring before
-- they arm its first lap, and `read` invalidates it between its snapshot
-- and its copy, so that every byte it copies was written before the line
-- it lies in was dropped.  The cacheless profile passes
-- `cache.cacheless(32)`, whose operations are the barriers alone, and the
-- cached profile `l1.new()`.  The contract the caller keeps: the ring
-- is whole lines of the provider's, which `open` refuses otherwise with
-- `cache.misaligned`; nothing but the stream writes it while it is open,
-- so it holds no dirty line once `open` or `restart` cleaned it, and the
-- laps the completion interrupt arms need no maintenance of their own;
-- and every call passes the provider `open` was given.  A `read` whose
-- provider refuses the ring breaks the descriptor, as a transfer error
-- does.
import core/cache
import core/line
import platform/cortex_m
import stm32h753/dma1
import stm32h753/dmamux1
import stm32h753/usart3

public busy: atom
public buffer_empty: atom
public buffer_too_big: atom
public bad_buffer: atom
public overrun: atom
public transfer_error: atom
public exhausted: atom
public stop_timeout: atom
public receive_error: type = overrun | transfer_error | exhausted | stop_timeout

public rx: type = struct
    buf: []u8
    consumed: u32
    budget: u32
    -- What the last snapshot counted, kept when the stream is stopped.
    produced: u32
    config: u32
    active: bool
    lost: bool
    broken: bool
    -- The bits of a received byte that are its frame's data, which read
    -- keeps: RDR holds the parity bit at the word's MSB, bit 7 of seven data
    -- bits and parity and bit 6 of six (48.5.12, Table 408, p. 2042; 48.8.12,
    -- p. 2098), and the stream moves it into the ring with them (D965).
    data_mask: u8
end rx

-- DMA_LISR and DMA_LIFCR for stream 0 (15.5.1, p. 659; 15.5.3, p. 660):
-- TCIF0 5, HTIF0 4, TEIF0 3, DMEIF0 2 and FEIF0 0.
transfer_complete: u32 = 0x20
completions: u32 = 0x30
errors: u32 = 0x0d
-- DMA_SxCR (15.5.5, p. 662): EN 0 and TCIE 4.
enable: u32 = 0x01
complete_interrupt: u32 = 0x10
-- USART_ISR and USART_ICR (48.8.9, p. 2085; 48.8.11, p. 2096): ORE and
-- ORECF, bit 3, and IDLE and IDLECF, bit 4.
uart_overrun: u32 = 0x08
uart_idle: u32 = 0x10
-- USART_ISR's TC, bit 6, and TXFE, bit 23 (48.8.9, p. 2085).
transmitted: u32 = 0x00800040

-- USART3's kernel clock, rcc_pclk1, the 64 MHz HSI (D647).
public clock: u32 = 64000000

-- What a line's settings program: BRR, CR1's frame bits, M1, M0, PCE and
-- PS, and CR2, whose STOP is its only field set.
public registers: type = struct
    divisor: u32
    frame: u32
    stop: u32
end registers

-- USART3's registers for SETTINGS, computed and written nowhere (D959).
-- Oversampling by 16, BRR is the divisor of the kernel clock, from 16 to
-- 65535 (48.5.7, p. 2034), so 977 to 4,000,000 baud at 64 MHz.  The word
-- is the data bits and the parity bit, 7, 8 or 9 of them, M1 28 and M0 12
-- giving 10, 00 and 01 (48.8.1, p. 2067); PCE 10 enables parity and PS 9
-- makes it odd; CR2's STOP, 13:12, is 00 for one stop bit and 10 for two
-- (48.8.3, p. 2074).  The receive ring and the transmitter move bytes, so
-- nine data bits are refused, and a word of five or six bits is outside
-- M: five data bits, and six without parity, are refused too.  115200
-- 8N1 is BRR 556, 0.08 percent fast, and no frame bit or stop bit set.
public line_registers: (settings: line.settings)
                       -> (images: registers) ! line.unsupported_rate | line.unsupported_format =
    try line.check(settings)
    mut word := u32(settings.data_bits)
    if settings.parity <> line.no_parity then word += 1 end if
    fail line.unsupported_format when settings.data_bits > 8 or word < 7
    value := try line.divisor(clock, settings.rate, 16, 4, 16, 65535)
    mut frame: u32 = 0
    if word == 7 then frame = 0x10000000 end if
    if word == 9 then frame = 0x00001000 end if
    if settings.parity == line.even_parity then frame |= 0x400 end if
    if settings.parity == line.odd_parity then frame |= 0x600 end if
    images = (divisor: value, frame: frame,
              stop: if settings.stop_bits == line.two_stop_bits then 0x2000 else 0 end if)
end line_registers

-- Program the line.  CR1's frame bits and FIFOEN, BRR and CR2 may be
-- written only with UE clear (48.8.1, p. 2067; 48.8.3, p. 2074; 48.8.5,
-- p. 2082), so CR1 is written first without it: RE 2, TE 3, IDLEIE 4 and
-- FIFOEN 29 beside the frame; then BRR, CR2 and CR3's DMAR, bit 6, which
-- receives through DMA; then CR1 again with UE, bit 0.
program: (images: registers) -> none =
    mut control1 := usart3.cr1_decode(images.frame)
    control1.f_re = 1
    control1.f_te = 1
    control1.f_idleie = 1
    control1.f_fifoen = 1
    usart3.cr1_write(usart3.base, usart3.cr1_encode(control1))
    usart3.brr_write(usart3.base, images.divisor)
    usart3.cr2_write(usart3.base, images.stop)
    mut control3 := usart3.cr3_decode(0)
    control3.f_dmar = 1
    usart3.cr3_write(usart3.base, usart3.cr3_encode(control3))
    control1.f_ue = 1
    usart3.cr1_write(usart3.base, usart3.cr1_encode(control1))
end program

-- What the completion interrupt and the descriptor's routines share, each
-- written in the interrupt or with interrupts masked: the bytes of the
-- laps this epoch has completed, the length of the lap armed, the epoch's
-- budget, the ring's size and address, the stream's configuration, and
-- whether another lap may be armed.
mut finished: u32 = 0
mut lap: u32 = 0
mut epoch: u32 = 0
mut ring: u32 = 0
mut ring_address: u32 = 0
mut stream_config: u32 = 0
mut arming: bool = false
-- Every lap and every epoch the stream has completed, for the trace.
mut laps_done: u32 = 0
mut epochs_done: u32 = 0

public laps: () -> (count: u32) = count = laps_done end laps

public epochs: () -> (count: u32) = count = epochs_done end epochs

-- Arm the next lap from the ring's start.  EN is clear, by the stream at
-- the end of the last lap or by stop, so its registers may be written,
-- and its completion flags are cleared before it is enabled again
-- (15.3.19, p. 656).
arm: () -> none =
    lap = ring
    if epoch -% finished < lap then lap = epoch -% finished end if
    dma1.s0m0ar_write(dma1.base, ring_address)
    dma1.s0ndtr_write(dma1.base, lap)
    dma1.lifcr_write(dma1.base, completions)
    cortex_m.device_barrier()
    dma1.s0cr_write(dma1.base, stream_config | enable)
end arm

-- DMA_STR0's handler calls this.  A lap that ended arms the next unless
-- the budget is reached or a stop cleared `arming`; an error stays in
-- DMA_LISR for the next snapshot, and its stream stays disabled.
public completed: () -> none =
    status := dma1.lisr_read(dma1.base)
    dma1.lifcr_write(dma1.base, status & completions)
    if (status & transfer_complete) <> 0 and (status & errors) == 0 and arming then
        finished = finished +% lap
        laps_done = laps_done +% 1
        if finished < epoch then
            arm()
        else
            lap = 0
            arming = false
            epochs_done = epochs_done +% 1
        end if
    end if
    cortex_m.device_barrier()
end completed

-- USART3's handler calls this: whether the line fell idle, IDLE cleared.
public idle: () -> (seen: bool) =
    status := usart3.isr_read(usart3.base)
    seen = (status & uart_idle) <> 0
    if seen then usart3.icr_write(usart3.base, uart_idle) end if
    cortex_m.device_barrier()
end idle

public open: (provider: type is cache.maintenance, caches: provider,
              settings: line.settings, budget: u32, escaping buf: []mut u8)
             -> (r: rx from buf) ! busy | line.unsupported_rate | line.unsupported_format
                                 | buffer_empty | buffer_too_big | bad_buffer
                                 | cache.misaligned =
    size := lenof buf
    fail buffer_empty when size == 0
    fail buffer_too_big when size > 256
    fail bad_buffer when size < 2 or (size & (size - 1)) <> 0
    fail bad_buffer when (usize(addr buf[0]) & (size - 1)) <> 0
    fail bad_buffer when budget < u32(size) or budget > 65535
    _ = try cache.lines(cache.line(caches), buf)
    images := try line_registers(settings)
    status := dma1.s0cr_read(dma1.base)
    fail busy when (status & enable) <> 0
      or (dma1.lisr_read(dma1.base) & errors) <> 0
    program(images)
    usart3.icr_write(usart3.base, uart_overrun | uart_idle)
    -- DMAMUX1's channel 0 serves DMA1 stream 0 (17.3.2, p. 696).
    mut request := dmamux1.dmamux_c0cr_decode(0)
    request.f_dmareq_id = 45
    dmamux1.dmamux_c0cr_write(dmamux1.base, dmamux1.dmamux_c0cr_encode(request))
    -- Peripheral to memory, bytes, memory increment, the completion
    -- interrupt and bufferable transfers; normal mode, direct mode,
    -- priority low.
    mut stream := dma1.s0cr_decode(0)
    stream.f_minc = 1
    stream.f_tcie = 1
    stream.f_trbuff = 1
    config := dma1.s0cr_encode(stream)
    dma1.s0par_write(dma1.base, u32(usart3.rdr_address))
    dma1.s0cr_write(dma1.base, config)
    -- D227's receive handoff: no dirty line of the ring left to be
    -- written over the stream's bytes, and none cached to hide them.
    try cache.clean_invalidate(caches, buf)
    saved := cortex_m.disable_interrupts()
    stream_config = config
    ring = u32(size)
    ring_address = u32(usize(addr buf[0]))
    epoch = budget
    finished = 0
    arming = true
    arm()
    cortex_m.restore_interrupts(saved)
    r = (buf: buf, consumed: 0, budget: budget, produced: 0, config: config,
         active: true, lost: false, broken: false,
         data_mask: line.data_mask(settings))
end open

-- The count never reloads in an epoch. Failure latches until an explicit
-- restart; a stopped descriptor answers from what its last snapshot saw.
snapshot: (inout r: rx) -> (count: usize) ! overrun | transfer_error | exhausted =
    if r.active then
        saved := cortex_m.disable_interrupts()
        status := dma1.lisr_read(dma1.base)
        remaining := dma1.s0ndtr_read(dma1.base)
        done := finished
        armed := lap
        cortex_m.restore_interrupts(saved)
        cortex_m.device_barrier()
        if (status & errors) <> 0 or remaining > armed then r.broken = true end if
        fail transfer_error when r.broken
        if (status & transfer_complete) <> 0 then
            r.produced = done +% armed
        else
            r.produced = done +% armed -% remaining
        end if
        if r.produced == r.budget then r.active = false end if
    end if
    if r.produced < r.consumed or r.produced > r.budget then r.broken = true end if
    fail transfer_error when r.broken
    have := r.produced -% r.consumed
    capacity := lenof r.buf
    if (usart3.isr_read(usart3.base) & uart_overrun) <> 0 then
        r.lost = true
        r.consumed = r.produced
    end if
    if have > u32(capacity) then
        r.lost = true
        r.consumed = r.produced
    end if
    fail overrun when r.lost
    fail exhausted when r.produced == r.budget and have == 0
    count = usize(have)
end snapshot

public available: (inout r: rx) -> (count: usize) ! receive_error =
    count = try snapshot(r)
end available

public read: (provider: type is cache.maintenance, caches: provider,
              inout r: rx, out_buf: []mut u8) -> (count: usize) ! receive_error =
    have := try snapshot(r)
    count = have
    if lenof out_buf < count then count = lenof out_buf end if
    backing := r.buf
    capacity := lenof backing
    -- D227's completion: the snapshot's count says the bytes are written,
    -- so the ring's lines are dropped before any of them is read.
    if count > 0 then
        cache.invalidate(caches, backing) else (problem)
            _ = problem
            r.broken = true
            fail transfer_error
        end
    end if
    -- snapshot bounds count by both initialized extents; consumed <= 65535,
    -- i < 256 and capacity is a nonzero power of two. No arithmetic wraps.
    unchecked begin
        for i in 0..<count do
            out_buf[i] = backing[(usize(r.consumed) + i) & (capacity - 1)] & r.data_mask
        end for
    end unchecked
    r.consumed = r.consumed +% u32(count)
end read

public stop: (inout r: rx) -> none ! stop_timeout =
    saved := cortex_m.disable_interrupts()
    defer cortex_m.restore_interrupts(saved)
    arming = false
    dma1.s0cr_write(dma1.base, stream_config & ~complete_interrupt)
    mut attempts: u32 = 0
    while (dma1.s0cr_read(dma1.base) & enable) <> 0 do
        attempts += 1
        fail stop_timeout when attempts == 1000
    end while
    if r.active then
        remaining := dma1.s0ndtr_read(dma1.base)
        if remaining <= lap then r.produced = finished +% lap -% remaining end if
    end if
    r.active = false
    dma1.lifcr_write(dma1.base, completions)
    cortex_m.device_barrier()
end stop

-- Discards unread bytes deliberately; may be called after error or exhaustion.
public restart: (provider: type is cache.maintenance, caches: provider, inout r: rx)
                -> none ! stop_timeout | transfer_error =
    try stop(r)
    status := dma1.lisr_read(dma1.base)
    fail transfer_error when (status & errors) <> 0
    cache.clean_invalidate(caches, r.buf) else (problem)
        _ = problem
        r.broken = true
        fail transfer_error
    end
    usart3.icr_write(usart3.base, uart_overrun)
    r.consumed = 0
    r.produced = 0
    r.lost = false
    r.broken = false
    r.active = true
    saved := cortex_m.disable_interrupts()
    dma1.s0cr_write(dma1.base, stream_config)
    finished = 0
    arming = true
    arm()
    cortex_m.restore_interrupts(saved)
end restart

-- Change the line to SETTINGS: every register image computed first, so a
-- refusal writes nothing and the line keeps its settings (D955).  Then,
-- once TC and TXFE say the transmitter has sent everything, the stream is
-- stopped, since clearing UE resets the USART's DMA requests (48.8.1,
-- p. 2067), the line programmed, and the stream restarted, which discards
-- what the ring held unread.  A byte arriving meanwhile is lost, so the
-- caller changes the line only while its peer is waiting for it.
public configure: (provider: type is cache.maintenance, caches: provider,
                   inout r: rx, settings: line.settings)
                  -> none ! line.unsupported_rate | line.unsupported_format
                          | stop_timeout | transfer_error =
    images := try line_registers(settings)
    while (usart3.isr_read(usart3.base) & transmitted) <> transmitted do
        continue
    end while
    try stop(r)
    program(images)
    r.data_mask = line.data_mask(settings)
    usart3.icr_write(usart3.base, uart_overrun | uart_idle)
    try restart(caches, r)
end configure
